---
// Two of the three labels here used to point at pages the build does not emit: `dist/client` contains
// index.html, chat/index.html and 404.html only, and public/ has no redirect rules — so "Schedule" and
// "Members" rendered the 404 page in production, on main as well as here. The operator ruled: drop them.
//
// What the row costs now, measured on the built page by `node design/nav-fit.mjs` — the same needed-vs-
// available arithmetic `check:render` runs, per label @measured 2026-11-14 (Linux native, Firefox 159.0,
// faces settled): the mark is 97px, `Schedule` 59, `Chat` 29, `Dates` 36, `Join` 24, `Attend` 42,
// `Ways in` 47, `Sign up` 47, `Meet` 32, `Visit` 28, `Get involved` 77. A row at the 320px floor (D114)
// has 276px of content box and 16px gaps, so the label set needs 217px and has 59px of headroom: a fourth
// label costs a gap plus a word no wider than 43px. `Schedule` + `Chat` + `Ways in` is 280 — four pixels
// over the floor, and `flex-nowrap` means that shows up as a failed probe rather than a silent wrap.
// Keeping `Ways in` therefore means paying for it with `Schedule` -> `Dates`: 97 + 36 + 29 + 47 + 3 gaps
// = 257, 19px inside the floor.
//
// Headroom, as it was measured before the schedule page existed, kept so a later reader does not re-argue it:
// the row was the mark plus `Chat` (171px of the 276 the floor offers), and `Schedule` at 57.6px would have
// fitted. It did, and 2026-11-07 spent that room on it (`src/pages/schedule.astro` emits
// `dist/client/schedule/index.html`) while `Members` stayed out for the reason D117 gives - no members page -
// not for lack of width. What the old note got wrong is the count: the floor had room for one more path, and
// the probe now says so every run.
// 2026-11-14 (S034, D308): the third path is the join page. The label is the page's own heading - "Ways in" -
// and `Schedule` came down to `Dates` to pay for it, because the floor cannot hold both words and the shorter
// candidates (`Join` 24, `Visit` 28, `Meet` 32, `Attend` 42 at 1px of margin) say less than the page does.
// Every label behind it is a page the build emits (D117), and `check:render` reports the shipped row at
// **257 needed of 276 offered** at 320 @measured 2026-11-14.
// The nav's subordinate date (2026-11-14, S036/D311): the commissioned read asked for "the next date in the
// nav". It is taken from the SAME call `/schedule` makes - `scheduleWindow(upcoming(), todayIn())` - so the
// chip cannot disagree with the page it points at; there is no second calendar in this file. `shown[0]` is the
// first date inside the window, which is exactly the first row the page paints.
//
// Width, measured on the built row by `node design/nav-fit.mjs --no-build` (Linux native, Firefox 159.0):
// `Dates` 36px, the chip `21 Nov` 41px, and the pair inside one anchor 80px. At the 320px floor (D114, 276px of
// content box, 16px gaps) the shipped row needs 257px; with the chip it needs 301px - 25px OVER the floor, and
// `flex-nowrap` means that is a failed probe, not a wrap. So the chip is hidden below the tier where it fits:
// `hidden xs:inline` with `--breakpoint-xs: 380px` in src/styles/global.css. It paints from 390 up - the row
// needs 306 of the 346 that width offers @measured 2026-11-15 by `npm run check:render:dist` - and stays hidden
// at the 320 floor, where the shipped row needs 257 of 276 and the same row with the chip needs 314, 38px over
// @measured 2026-11-15 by `node design/nav-fit.mjs --no-build`. The round-3 read (§12 criterion 5) asked for the chip
// at 390px; `sm` (640) was hiding it at a width the measurement says can hold it.
// 2026-11-16 (D373): the route the `Dates` label points at moved from `/schedule` to `/dates`. The label never
// said `schedule` — the nav, the band and the page's own h1 all said `Dates` while the URL said something else,
// which is the defect the round-5 read priced as Batch D; the words and the path now match.
import { upcoming, dateParts, formatDate } from '../lib/content.ts';
import { todayIn } from '../lib/events.mjs';
import { scheduleWindow } from '../lib/event-window.mjs';

const window = scheduleWindow(upcoming(), todayIn());
// `null` when the calendar is empty or every open date is past the window: the nav then says nothing it cannot
// back up, and no `data-nav-date` is emitted for check:dated-surfaces to audit.
const nextDate = window.shown[0]?.date ?? null;

const navItems = [
  { href: '/dates', label: 'Dates', date: nextDate },
  { href: '/chat', label: 'Chat' },
  // Round-6 review (2026-11-16), `/join`/Revise (D390): the third label is `Join`, not `Ways in`. A first-timer
  // scans for a verb they can act on, and `check:naming`'s name-not-on-page rule is what ties the label to the
  // page: `/join`'s h1 is now "Join the club". The width this buys is measured, not guessed - see the note below
  // the row, which `node design/nav-fit.mjs` re-prices every run.
  { href: '/join', label: 'Join' },
];

// The chip's own words, in the order the anchor paints them. `chipWords` is used twice on the date-bearing
// item - in the anchor's `aria-label` and in the chip's text - so the name and the page cannot drift apart
// the next time the short form changes (D333, 2026-11-15).
const chipWords = (iso: string | null) => (iso ? `${dateParts(iso).day} ${dateParts(iso).month}` : '');
---

<!-- Round 17 (MS03), operator ruling 2026-11-09 (S017 B1): the bar is chrome by an edge, not by a ground.
     Measured on the built page before the change (2026-11-08, design/grill/edge-probe.mjs): the header paints
     no ground of its own - its computed background is the page's paper (layer contrast 1.00, OKLab distance 0),
     so nothing in the bar separates it from the page. Every ground the palette has was priced and refused:
     panel 1.04, tint 1.10, warm tint 1.11 (drops `rule` to 2.74 against its own 3.0 bar), warm band 1.20
     (`rule` 2.51), accent fill fails outright (nav ink 2.5) — those are the numbers the round priced with the
     palette it had. The ratified inset ring is still the one device that clears the graphic bar for free in
     height: **3.66** against the paper it stands on @measured 2026-11-14 by `npm run check:contrast`, bar
     still 1228x72.
     `shadow-e2` paints 1.78 - a layer, not an edge - and is held back until the bar actually moves (sticky,
     parked in PLAN.md §Parked with its trigger). An inset ring is not a border (D103), so check:design's
     no-border rule still holds.

     S027 / D295 (operator ruling B1, 2026-11-13): the bar is chrome by its **ring alone**. `bg-paper` was
     deleted from the header class - it declared a ground identical to the page behind it (re-measured by
     `node design/round17.mjs --only shipped`, 2026-11-13), so it painted nothing and invited a future reader
     to think the bar has a surface of its own. The ring is the whole device; sticky is still parked. -->
<header class="inset-shadow-rule">
  <nav class="max-w-screen-xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-4">
    <!-- D100 shape B, re-measured on the built artifact by `npm run check:render`: one row at every
         width, `flex-wrap: nowrap`, one type tier (text-label) at every width, 16px gaps, and link
         targets 32px tall (16px line box + py-2). D100 rejected a 13px target, so the pad is
         load-bearing — do not shave it.

         `flex-nowrap` is the load-bearing part. The shipped nav was `flex-col` + `flex-wrap`, which is
         why the page clipped and links disappeared (A needed 397px, more than every content box this
         site ships). With nowrap, a label set that stops fitting shows up as a failed width probe
         instead of a silent re-wrap. -->
    <div class="flex flex-nowrap items-center gap-4">
      <a
        href="/"
        class="flex-shrink-0 whitespace-nowrap py-2 font-body text-body text-ink"
        aria-label="Matheapolis, home"
      >
        Matheapolis
      </a>
      <div class="flex flex-nowrap items-center gap-4">
        {navItems.map(item => (
          <a
            href={item.href}
            class="whitespace-nowrap py-2 text-label text-ink font-medium hover:text-accent"
            aria-label={item.date ? `${item.label} ${chipWords(item.date)}, next meeting ${formatDate(item.date, 'wide')}` : item.label}
          >
            {item.label}
            {/* The subordinate date, not a second nav item. `data-nav-date` is the handle
                scripts/check-dated-surfaces.mjs reads: a chrome date is allowed only when it equals the first
                date the window says the schedule page prints (D311), so the nav can never print a stale date
                the page of record does not. Hidden below `sm` for the measured 25px the pair needs at the
                320px floor. The gap in front of the chip is a TEXT NODE, not ml-2: Astro ate the newline between
                the two expressions, so the anchor serialized as >Dates<span data-nav-date=... and the two visible
                words were one word - which is what axe-core's label-in-name rule (SC 2.5.7) called on all four
                pages, and a margin says nothing to a screen reader anyway (D333, measured 2026-11-15 by
                node design/axe-check.mjs).

                The accessible name is a sentence, not the two visible words: `Dates 21 Nov, next meeting Saturday, Nov 21`.
                It has to *begin* with the words a visitor sees: SC 2.5.7 ("label in name") asks that an element's
                accessible name contain its visible text, and axe-core's `label-content-name-mismatch` - serious
                impact, flagged on the nav chip of all four pages @measured 2026-11-15 by `node design/axe-check.mjs` -
                is that rule as a check. The old name put the chip's words nowhere (`Dates, next meeting Saturday, Nov 21`),
                so a reader who activates the link by name could not find it by what it says. The weekday still cannot go
                in the visible chip - `node design/nav-fit.mjs --no-build` @measured 2026-11-14 prices `Sat 21 Nov` at
                286px against a 276px content box at the 320 floor (D311) - but it costs nothing in the name, and a chip
                that reads "21 Nov" to a screen reader is a number with no subject. The name is checked by the same gate,
                rule nav-chip-name-thin. With no date on the calendar the anchor falls back to the plain label: no
                sentence about a meeting that does not exist. */}
            {item.date && (
              <span data-nav-date={item.date} class="hidden xs:inline text-label text-ink-3 tabular-nums">
                {' '}{chipWords(item.date)}
              </span>
            )}
          </a>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </nav>
</header>
